<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_risk_perc" target="energy_asset_realloc::capital_inflows_oil" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising expectations of rapid escalation increase sovereign and project risk premia and prompt lenders, insurers and equity providers to delay or withdraw financing for upstream/midstream oil projects.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_asset_realloc</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_risk_perc" target="global_bond_repricing::market_implied_rates" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher perceived escalation increases the probability of major energy/commodity shocks or prolonged supply disruption, which pushes up inflation expectations and leads markets to price a higher short‑term policy‑rate path.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_risk_perc" target="us_china_tech_trade::regulatory_stringency" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising perceptions that the Russia–Ukraine war could escalate prompt governments to tighten export controls, investment screening and sanctions coverage for dual‑use AI and semiconductor technologies to prevent transfer to adversaries, raising regulatory strictness on US–China tech flows.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_trade</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_risk_perc" target="us_china_tech_trade::chip_production_capacity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher perceived escalation drives multilateral denial policies plus private‑sector de‑risking (insurance, equipment vendors, finance) that restrict shipment of advanced semiconductor tools and investment into Chinese fabs, reducing China's producible chip capacity over the near term.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_trade</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_risk_perc" target="us_iran_confront::shipping_throughput" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising perception that the Ukraine conflict could spiral increases insurance premiums and risk-avoidance by commercial ship operators, causing vessels to reroute away from Middle Eastern chokepoints and reducing throughput through those sea lanes.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_confront</data>
</edge>
